3. Core Language/Framework Philosophy
Nothing hardcoded — every technique, layer, bridge, optimizer, and visualization is defined via an open interface, not baked into the compiler
Interpretability by default — every model type supports inspection/explanation out of the box; "black box" is opt-in, not the default
Fewer lines than Python — no heavy import chains; common techniques/layers available with zero imports
Compiled to C via Rust — fast, with compile-time shape/architecture checking
The `Technique` Interface
```
technique Technique {
    fn forward(input) -> output
    fn objective(output, target) -> Signal   // generic: loss, reward, distance — technique decides
    fn update(signal)
    fn dashboard() -> DashboardLayout        // technique declares its own visualization
}
```
Built-ins (Regression, CNN, Transformer, MoE, Diffusion, etc.) are just pre-shipped implementations of this same interface — no special compiler treatment. Users write their own the same way.
```
technique MyNewTechnique extends Regression {
    override fn objective(output, target) { /* custom math */ }
}
```
`objective()` returns a generic `Signal` — not every technique uses "loss" (RL uses reward, diffusion uses denoising error, GANs use two competing losses, K-Means has no weights/loss at all)
Same open-interface pattern applies to: custom layers, custom bridges (model merging), custom visualizations, custom optimizers
Fork-and-Protect Boilerplate System
```
qai new technique --from="Regression" --name="MyRegression"
qai new layer --from="Attention" --name="MyAttention"
qai new bridge --from="Adapter" --name="MyBridge"
```
Originals stay immutable in the core library; you always work on a named copy. Prevents accidentally breaking a working technique while still allowing full customization.
Formula Visualization
```
Regression.show_formula()          // renders y = mx + b with live parameter values
MyRegression.diff(Regression)      // shows exactly what math changed, readable notation
```
