20. Weight-to-Words & Educational Visualization
```
model.weights.translate()   // "0.803, -0.001, 0.42..." → "strongly associates with 'positive sentiment'"
```
What a learner should be able to see, per concept:
Probability — live bar chart of all candidate outputs and their scores, not just the winner
Regression — literal scatter plot + fitted line redrawing itself each epoch, residual lines shrinking visibly
Classification — 2D decision boundary redrawing live; confusion matrix filling in during training
Neural nets generally — node/edge diagram with edges lighting up by weight magnitude; forward-pass animation showing values transforming layer by layer
Gradient descent — a loss-landscape visual with a "ball rolling downhill," the physical intuition behind the math
Per-technique flagship visuals — CNN: filter maps; RNN: unrolled timestep view; Transformer: attention heatmaps; MoE: expert routing grid; GAN: generator/discriminator score tug-of-war; Diffusion: step-by-step denoising reveal
Dashboard design principle: each technique declares its own `dashboard()` (unique panels like scatter plots or denoising previews), but shares common building blocks (loss curve, gradient health) from a shared visual library. Techniques without weights (e.g. K-Means) simply omit weight-related panels.
Layer parameters explained (example):
`embedding(size: 512)` — turns tokens into 512-number vectors the model can do math on
`moe(experts: 32, active: 4)` — 32 specialized sub-networks exist, only 4 run per input (router-selected)
`classifier(output: 3)` — produces 3 probability scores, one per category
Tokens: text is split into tokens (words/sub-words) before embedding — `qai.tokenize("hello world", method: "bpe")`.
