32. Historical / Foundational Techniques (for teaching AI history)
Runnable as real techniques with the same interface as modern ones — not just documentation — so a course can build a 1958 perceptron and a 2026 MoE transformer with the same three lines of code:
```
let model = qai.build(technique: "perceptron")
```
Perceptron (1958) — original single-layer neural unit, ancestor of deep learning
Symbolic AI / expert systems — rule-based "if-then" reasoning, pre-statistical-ML era
K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) — classify by proximity to labeled examples
Decision stumps / ID3 decision trees — original tree-splitting algorithms, precursors to Random Forest/XGBoost
Hidden Markov Models (HMM) — sequence modeling before RNNs (original backbone of speech recognition)
Bayesian Networks — probabilistic graphical models for reasoning under uncertainty
Genetic algorithms / evolutionary computation — optimization via natural-selection-style search instead of gradients
Self-Organizing Maps (Kohonen networks) — early unsupervised clustering/visualization technique
Hopfield Networks & Boltzmann Machines — early recurrent/energy-based models, precursors to modern generative models
