37. Model-to-Model Piping & Full Modality List
```
let model_a = qai.build(technique: "transformer")
let model_b = qai.build(technique: "diffusion", output_type: "image")

model_a.generate("describe a scene").pipe_to(model_b).render_as("image")
```
`.pipe_to()` reuses the merge/bridge system inline, with the same compatibility check as `render_as()` — any number of models can chain (text → image → video → 3D), or loop back into themselves for iterative refinement.
Native modality list: Text, Image, Video, Audio, 3D (mesh/point cloud), Documents (PDF/DOCX/spreadsheets), Tabular/structured data, Time-series data, Graph/network data, Action sequences (robot/game control), raw Embeddings/vectors — plus fully custom renderer types (e.g. legal contracts, CAD files, sheet music) via the same open `OutputRenderer` interface, not limited to this built-in list.
Blind spots identified:
Streaming/partial output — `model.generate_stream(prompt, on_chunk: fn(piece) {...})` needed for token-by-token or frame-by-frame generation
Pipeline cycle safety — feedback loops between chained models need a default `max_iterations`/cycle-detection guard
Cross-model format mismatches — finer than capability matching (e.g. 512px vs 1024px images) needs lightweight auto-adapters at the pipe level
Whole-pipeline tracing — `pipeline.trace()` to show input/output at every hop across a multi-model chain, since per-model `.explain()`/`.watch()` don't cover cross-model chains
Pipeline cost/latency estimation — `pipeline.estimate_cost()` before running a long chain
Backpressure handling — defined behavior (queue/buffer/block) when one model in a chain is much slower than others
