40. Low-Memory Inference & Lazy Loading
Modeled after real tools like AirLLM (open-source, streams model layers/experts instead of loading the whole model into memory, letting 70B+ models run on 4GB VRAM without quantization) and llama.cpp/GGUF (CPU-friendly quantized inference), vLLM (memory-efficient serving), ONNX Runtime/ExecuTorch (cross-platform/mobile), Ollama (one-command local run UX).
```
let model = qai.load("huge-model",
    strategy: "layer_stream",   // one layer resident in memory at a time, unload after use
    prefetch: true)              // next layer loads while current one computes, hides latency

model.load(auto_adapt: true)     // picks full-load / layer-streaming / quantized-load automatically
                                  // based on qai.detect_hardware()
```
Disk-as-extended-memory fallback when RAM/VRAM is insufficient
`model.load_report()` — shows expected speed tradeoff of layer-streaming vs full load before committing
Partial GPU/CPU offload — split a model across both simultaneously rather than all-or-nothing
---
41. Model Memory System
Memory means different things in real AI systems, and Quantum should map onto the real distinction rather than a single generic idea:
```
model.memory.weights        // baked-in, learned via training — slow/permanent, changes only via finetune
model.memory.working        // current context window — fast, temporary, per-session
model.memory.retrieval       // external store (vector-DB style) — fast to update, no retraining required
    .add("fact")
    .search(query)
```
Weights = long-term but static memory (what training compressed in)
Working memory = the context window, cleared per session unless explicitly carried forward
Retrieval memory = how real production systems give a model fast-changing "long-term memory" without retraining (RAG-style) — this is the actual answer to "can a model use storage as memory"
More memory ideas:
Memory conflict resolution — `model.memory.retrieval.resolve_conflicts()` flags contradicting stored facts
Memory expiry/TTL — `add("fact", expires: "30d")` for time-sensitive info
Auto memory-strategy picker — decides whether new info belongs in retrieval (fast-changing) vs triggers a fine-tune (stable skills)
Knowledge freshness report — `model.memory.staleness_report()` flags likely-outdated trained-in facts
