56. Type/Technique Authoring System
`type` and `technique` are interchangeable keywords — same concept, whichever reads naturally.
Every Type decomposes into swappable mechanics, not one fixed formula:
```
type Regression {
    structure { layers: [linear] }                  // what makes it "this" Type specifically

    uses optimizer: GradientDescent                   // swappable: Adam, SGD, Lion...
    uses objective: MSE                                // swappable: MAE, Huber, CrossEntropy...
    uses training_loop: RetrainUntil(loss < 0.1)       // swappable: Once, EpochBased(n), TimeBoxed(duration)
}

type MyRegression extends Regression {
    uses optimizer: Adam
    uses training_loop: RetrainUntil(loss < 0.05, max_attempts: 10)
}
```
`Optimizer`, `Objective`, and `TrainingLoop` are their own open interfaces/registries, shared across every Type — a CNN and a transformer can both use `GradientDescent` + `RetrainUntil(...)` while keeping their own unique `structure`
Mechanics compatibility check — e.g. a non-differentiable Type can't use a gradient-based optimizer; errors clearly rather than failing silently
"Mixing board" dashboard view — swap optimizer/objective/loop live and watch the effect, rather than editing code
Named loop presets — `TrainingLoop.retrain_until_convergence()`, `.retrain_n_times(5)`, `.train_once()`
Full Type definition anatomy:
```
type Regression {
    meta { name: "Regression", family: "classical_ml", description: "...", version: "1.0" }

    capabilities { input: ["tabular","numeric"], output: ["numeric_prediction"], supports_weights: true }

    params {
        learning_rate: f64 { alias: "learning_speed", default: 0.01, range: [0.0001, 1.0] }
    }

    fn forward(input) -> output { ... }
    fn objective(output, target) -> Signal { ... }
    fn update(signal) { ... }

    on_init { ... }
    on_epoch_end { ... }
    on_train_end { ... }

    fn formula() -> Equation { ... }        // unique method

    validate {
        case("simple linear data") { train_on(known_linear_dataset); assert(accuracy > 0.95) }
    }
}
```
Each block auto-populates a different system: `meta` → registry/family grouping; `capabilities` → Input/Output matching (Section 44); `params` → `.help()`, `qai.glossary()`, dashboard cheat cards (Section 45); lifecycle hooks → dashboard/autosave/anomaly alerts wire in automatically; `validate` → required before a Type is marked verified.
Lifecycle states: `draft → validated (passes validate{}) → certified (benchmarked, Section 25) → published (registry) → deprecated`. `type.status()` shows current state; unverified custom types are flagged visibly wherever they're used.
Composition:
```
type MyRegression extends Regression { ... }
type Hybrid combine(CNN, Transformer) { ... }
type Rigging requires(MeshGeneration) { ... }
```
Additional Type families worth adding as real built-ins:
`SpeculativeDecoding { draft_model, verifier }` — fast draft model proposes tokens, larger model verifies in one pass
Meta-learning / few-shot (MAML-style) — learns how to learn quickly from very few examples
Causal inference — cause-and-effect estimation, distinct from correlation-based prediction
Anomaly detection — first-class family for fraud/defect/outlier detection
Learning-to-rank — search/recommendation-specific, optimizes order not a single prediction
Time-series forecasting — seasonality/trend-aware tools, distinct from generic regression
