60. Unified Input/Output System (Settled Version)
No separate "transform" concept — everything routes through the same `Input`/`Output` pair, whether at training-data-prep time or live inference time. Invisible and zero-config by default; only surfaced when a developer actively wants control.
```
// Zero config — the default, for everyone who doesn't need to touch endpoints
model.train(data)
model.generate(prompt)

// Training-time shaping — model genuinely LEARNS the pattern, not cosmetic
data.on_input(fn(example) { example.image = example.image.crop(region).resize(256,256); return example })
data.on_output(fn(example) { example.output = example.output.upper(); return example })
model.train(data)

// Inference-time — same two endpoint names
model.input("photo.jpg", as: "image")
model.generate(prompt).output("json" | "plain_text" | "markdown")
```
Optional, dial-able validation at both endpoints — never forced:
```
model.input.validate(level: "none" | "basic" | "strict")
model.output.validate(level: "none" | "basic" | "strict")
```
`basic` is the sane default everywhere; `strict` (full Section 33/35 security scanning) is opt-in for production/security-sensitive cases, since an aggressive default could silently reject legitimate unusual input and frustrate beginners.
`.chat()` — instant auto-generated UI, no HTML/JS written by hand:
```
model.chat()   // opens a real browser chat window, or an upload-and-predict form for a classifier, etc.
```
This is the simplest case of `qai.dashboard()` (Section 19) — the framework already knows the model's input/output shape and generates the right interface automatically.
Multi-layer/mixed-type models — still one door in, one door out from the outside:
```
let model = qai.build()
model.add_layer("rnn_part", type: "rnn")
model.add_layer("transformer_part", type: "transformer")
model.add_layer("regression_head", type: "regression")
model.connect("rnn_part" -> "transformer_part" -> "regression_head")

model.input(raw_data)      // ONE entry point for the whole model
model.generate(prompt)      // ONE exit point — internal layers stay private
```
Advanced, opt-in internal taps remain available using the exact same vocabulary, applied at a deeper level:
```
model.layer("transformer_part").input.watch()
model.layer("rnn_part").output.watch()
```
Supporting checks:
`model.check_endpoints()` — verifies training-time input/output shaping matches what's expected at inference time, catching train/serve mismatches
`model.endpoint_log()` — one place listing every input/output manipulation ever applied, across both training prep and live serving
