71. Understanding Model Logic (Plain Definitions)
Logic = the actual steps a model follows to turn input into output — not the learned numbers (weights/parameters) and not the settings (hyperparameters), just the plan of action itself. Weights are closer to the neurons and their connection strengths (the hardware); logic is closer to the pattern of thinking that uses them (the process) — a useful, if simplified, analogy.
Per-modality examples of what "logic" plainly means:
Text: "Look at the words so far. Guess the most likely next word. Say it. Repeat."
Video: "Look at the current frame. Predict the next frame. Check it matches the character's appearance. Output it. Repeat."
Audio: "Look at the sound so far. Predict the next tiny slice. Smooth it so it doesn't click. Add it. Repeat."
Image (diffusion): "Start with noise. Guess what noise to remove. Remove a little. Repeat until clear."
Real model logic contains genuine control flow — loops, if-statements, boolean conditions — not simplified one-liners:
```
fn forward(input) {
    let x = embed(input)
    for layer in self.layers {
        x = attention(x)
        x = x + residual(x)
        if self.use_normalization { x = normalize(x) }
    }
    if confidence(x) > 0.95 { return x }   // early exit if already confident
    return final_layer(x)
}
```
Real precedent: MoE routing (`if gate_score(expert) > threshold: send_to(expert)`) is a genuine if-statement inside real production model logic (Mixtral/DeepSeek-style models).
Reality check on adoption: most AI engineers never touch logic — they use an existing architecture and tune settings/data. Logic-writing is what researchers do when inventing new architectures. Quantum's `forward()`/`override` system exists to make that second, smaller group's work far more accessible — not to replace the first group's simpler, and equally valid, workflow.
---
72. Two-Tier Logic System: Deep Logic vs. Composition Logic
Level 1 — Deep Logic: rewriting the actual math inside a Type's core methods.
```
type Regression { fn forward(x) { return dot(w, x) + b } }                       // a straight line
type PolynomialRegression extends Regression {
    override fn forward(x) { return dot(w, x^2) + dot(w2, x) + b }                // genuinely different math — a curve
}
```
Level 2 — Composition Logic: wiring a model's already-exposed methods together into new behavior, with zero math required — the more accessible, much more commonly useful tier:
```
let frame = model.predict_next_frame(current_frame)
if model.penalty(frame) > threshold { frame = model.predict_next_frame(current_frame, retry: true) }
model.output(frame)
model.input(frame)   // feeding output back into input — new behavior from existing pieces alone
```
Combining both — train a model until it internalizes a composed procedure, so the scripted version is no longer needed at inference time:
```
fn generate_long_video(model, target_duration) {
    let output = []
    let current = model.generate_first_chunk()
    while output.total_duration() < target_duration {
        let next = model.predict_next_chunk(current, duration: 3s)
        if model.consistency_score(current, next) < 0.8 {
            next = model.predict_next_chunk(current, duration: 3s, retry: true)
        }
        output.append(next)
        current = next
    }
    return output
}

model.train(
    scripted_procedure: generate_long_video,
    until: fn(result) { result.consistency_score > 0.95 && result.duration >= target_duration }
)
// after training, the model naturally does this — no scripted retry loop needed at inference time
```
This maps to real research concepts: process supervision (training toward a defined multi-step success condition) and distilling a scripted procedure into an end-to-end model (self-play systems like AlphaGo use the same basic shape).
Honest caveat, kept as a standing note: making a technique easy to express doesn't make the underlying problem easy — long-horizon consistency (e.g. a 3-hour consistent video) remains a genuinely hard open research problem regardless of how well-designed the training loop is. The framework makes the technique accessible; it doesn't make the technique magic.
Supporting tools:
`model.landing_progress()` — how close training is to reliably satisfying the `until` condition
Partial-credit tracking — shows where a near-miss result degrades, not just pass/fail
`model.compare_to_scripted(procedure)` — how the model's now-natural behavior differs from the original manual version
