74. The `model.logic{}` Block, Full Method Reference & Modality Traits
Block syntax — entry and exit are mandatory, since a logic block with no defined start/end isn't checkable or meaningful:
```
import qai
let story_model = qai.build(type: "transformer")
let video_model = qai.build(type: "diffusion", output_type: "video")

model.logic {
    input(prompt)                                        // ENTRY — required

    let story = story_model.generate(prompt)
    let video = video_model.generate(story)

    if video_model.penalty() > threshold {
        video_model.learning.penalty += 5                // reaching into a technique-specific property from inside logic
    }

    if video.quality() > 0.9 {
        model.online_learn(from: video)                    // self-improves from its own best outputs
    }

    output(video)                                          // EXIT — required
}
```
Using a logic block is entirely optional — the plain path (`qai.build()` → `train()` → done) needs no logic block at all and remains just as simple as ever.
Full method reference, three tiers:
General (every model):
```
model.train()  .finetune()  .test()  .predict()  .generate()  .input()  .output()
model.loss()  .accuracy()  .parameters()  .hyperparameters()  .weights()
model.save() / .load()  .export()  .explain()  .visualize()  .help()
model.eval_mode() / .train_mode()   // behavior differs during training vs. real use for some techniques
model.to(device) / .device()  .clone()  .summary()  .size()
model.freeze_layer(name) / .unfreeze_layer(name)  .checkpoint() / .rollback()  .state()
```
Type-specific (recap, Section 46): `.formula()` (Regression), `.filter_visualize()` (CNN), `.attention_map()` (Transformer), `.expert_routing()` (MoE), `.denoise_steps()` (Diffusion) — plus deeper editable internals:
```
Regression:   .set_coefficient(i, value)  .lock_intercept()  .add_feature()  .remove_feature()
CNN:          .set_filter_weights(layer, values)  .freeze_filter(i)  .visualize_activation(layer)
Transformer:  .set_attention_head_weight(head, value)  .mask_token(pos)  .set_top_p()  .set_top_k()
```
Technique/learning-paradigm-specific (Section 67): `.reward` / `.penalty` (Reinforcement), `.source_model` / `.frozen_layers` (Transfer), `.generate_own_labels()` (Self-supervised) — all reachable and manipulable from inside a logic block.
Modality traits — a third, separate axis from Type: a model's declared input/output modality grants automatic methods regardless of its internal architecture:
```
// ANY video-output model gets these, whether built on Diffusion, GAN, or anything else
model.frame_count()  model.duration()  model.fps()  model.extract_frame(n)  model.frame_rate(30)
```
Multi-objective training — training on more than one paradigm at once, genuinely possible:
```
model.train(data, objectives: [
    { type: "supervised", weight: 0.7 },
    { type: "reinforcement", weight: 0.3 }
])
```
This mirrors real practice — e.g. RLHF-style training genuinely combines supervised pretraining with reinforcement learning layered on top.
Real-world tweaking without any logic at all (most common real AI development, worth keeping visible alongside the advanced system): a developer might just lower `temperature` from 0.7 to 0.3 for more predictable output, or move a classifier's decision threshold from 0.5 to 0.7 to reduce false positives — zero architecture change, zero logic, just a dial turned.
Supporting tools:
```
qai.compare_outputs([a.output(), b.output()]).show()   // instant simple side-by-side view, no HTML/CSS/JS needed
logic.help()                                              // lists everything reachable at this exact point in a logic block
model.has_logic()                                          // quick check: plain model or custom logic attached?
```
`.explain()` on a model with custom logic also shows which part of the logic influenced a given output, not just which weights did.
