77. Logic System: Capabilities and Honest Limits
Real, substantial wins — not just marketing:
RAM waste — `load_region()` loading only relevant weights per request
Storage waste — memory-triage logic avoids hoarding everything indefinitely
The black-box/no-control problem — before logic, a model ran one fixed way with no way to say "output this," "retrain now," "skip this part"; logic genuinely removes that ceiling
Automating iterative improvement — a written training loop can run, evaluate, and adjust without manual restarts
Confirmed behavior for models without logic attached:
```
model.export("plain_model.gguf")   // no logic — behaves exactly like a normal, non-Quantum model, nothing lost
model.accuracy()                    // testing/inspection methods work with zero logic block, always have
```
A downloaded third-party model (e.g. an open-weight model from outside the ecosystem) arrives with no Quantum logic attached by default — plain fine-tuning works exactly as normal; logic is always something you add, never something a foreign model brings with it.
Honest limits — what logic does NOT automatically solve:
"Infinite loop = infinitely smarter" is not quite right. An unattended self-retraining loop only genuinely improves the model if its evaluator is trustworthy and can't be gamed. Reward hacking — a model finding a way to satisfy the check without actually improving (trivial, narrow, or overfit answers that technically pass) — is a real, well-documented failure mode, and it's easiest to miss precisely when no one is watching the loop.
Compute/cost is real and unbounded without limits — "let it run forever getting better" also means "let it run forever costing money and energy."
Debugging gets genuinely harder — a model that self-modified many times is much harder to reason about than a static one.
Reproducibility gets harder — if weights keep changing via self-directed logic, "the exact model that produced this result" becomes a moving target, in tension with the reproducibility fingerprinting idea (Section 26).
Safety mechanisms that make the power trustworthy, not just powerful:
```
model.detect_reward_hacking()   // periodically tests against a protected golden eval set (Section 55) to catch gaming vs. real progress
model.self_improve(pause_every: 100, require_human_review: true)   // long loops can't run forever unattended
model.session_summary()          // plain-language account of what changed and why, after a long self-directed run
```
Bottom line: the RAM/storage/automation wins are real and substantial. The self-improvement power needs budgets, audit trails, and golden-eval checks treated as load-bearing parts of the feature, not optional add-ons — this exact combination (self-modifying weights + unattended loops) is where real AI systems have historically gone wrong in ways that are hard to notice until later.
