79. Router Core & Predictive Loading
Correction worth keeping explicit: less RAM used does not automatically mean faster — it's normally a tradeoff. Loading weights on-demand from disk saves memory but adds latency; the real win is avoiding loading things that are never needed, not "less RAM = inherently faster."
A small, always-resident "router core" is required to know what to load before loading it:
```
let router = model.router_core()                          // small, cheap, ALWAYS in RAM
let target_region = router.predict_needed_region(prompt)
let active = model.load_region(target_region)               // only the heavy weights load, based on the router's cheap prediction
```
This mirrors how real MoE routing already works internally, generalized to whole-model region loading.
Predictive prefetching based on conversation trajectory:
```
model.predictive_prefetch(based_on: conversation_trajectory, confidence_threshold: 0.7)
// loads the likely-next-topic region in the background while the current response is still being read
```
Where `model.memory` actually lives — three different physical realities behind one API:
```
model.memory.weights      // disk normally; streamed into RAM/VRAM only when actively needed (Section 40)
model.memory.working       // RAM only — the context window, transient
model.memory.retrieval     // disk/external DB by default; specific retrieved pieces pulled into RAM briefly

model.memory.location_report()   // → { weights: "disk, streamed", working: "ram, 1.2k tokens", retrieval: "disk, 40k entries" }
```
Supporting tools:
`router.accuracy_report()` — how often the router's region guess was actually correct
`model.set_memory_strategy("max_speed" | "min_ram" | "balanced")` — an explicit, visible tradeoff dial
`model.expect_slower_first_response()` — honest cold-start expectation, since prefetching needs trajectory history it doesn't have yet on the first request
