81. Logic's Dual Mechanism & Honest Agency Clarification
Logic can trigger both weight edits and logic swaps — two distinct mechanisms:
```
model.logic {
    input(x)
    if model.confidence(x) < 0.5 {
        model.weights.set(layer: "layer3", index: [12,45], value: 0.91)      // MECHANISM A — change the numbers
    }
    if model.performance_report().shows_pattern("always wrong on negatives") {
        model.swap_logic(ImprovedForwardLogic)                                 // MECHANISM B — change the steps themselves
    }
    output(model.generate(x))
}
```
Honest clarification, worth keeping explicit in the docs: logic does not give a model self-awareness or self-control over its own weights. A model only ever produces outputs (like a confidence score); the developer's pre-written logic decides what to do in response to that output. It looks like self-directed behavior, but precisely: the model produces a signal, and logic — written in advance by a person — reacts to it. This is a real and valuable capability; it is not the model gaining agency.
Weights: flat in memory, structured when accessed — both true, at different levels:
```
model.weights.raw_flat()    // the actual underlying flat array (what hardware wants)
model.weights.as_table()     // the same data reshaped into named/labeled structure (what a person wants)
```
Same relationship as a spreadsheet file (flat bytes on disk, rows/columns when opened).
Supporting tools:
Change-source labeling — every change tagged with why it happened (`triggered_by: "low_confidence_rule"`), feeding `weight_history()`
`model.correlate_logic_to_weights()` — which logic rules caused the most weight changes over time
A dashboard panel plainly separating "the model's raw output" from "logic's decision based on that output"
