84. Explicit Exits, Operation Feedback & Relational Weights (Open Research)
Logic never implicitly "knows" when to stop — every exit is an explicit statement:
```
model.logic {
    input(x)
    var attempts = 0
    while attempts < 10 {
        let result = model.generate(x)
        if result.quality() > 0.9 {
            output(result)   // this line IS how it "knows" — an explicit statement, not inference
            return
        }
        attempts += 1
    }
    output(result)            // fallback exit, always reachable
}
```
This is exactly why hard iteration caps (Sections 69/77) are mandatory, not optional — nothing else stops a badly-written loop from never reaching an `output()`.
SQL-style operation confirmations — any state-changing call prints a plain summary of what happened, by default:
```
qai.transplant_weights(from: model_a, region: "coding_capability", to: model)
// → "245 values changed. Region 'coding_capability' transplanted from model_a to model at 2026-08-29 14:02:11."

model.finetune(data)
// → "Fine-tune complete. 1,200 weights updated across 3 layers. Accuracy: 0.87 → 0.91."
```
Variable naming = instance identity — no special mechanism needed, ordinary programming:
```
let code_model = qai.build(type: "transformer")
let image_model = qai.build(type: "diffusion")
code_model.train(coding_data)     // unambiguous — a method call on this specific named instance
```
Re-running a script mid-debugging never forces a full retrain — `if_exists: "resume"` (Section 42) is exactly the mechanism:
```
let model = qai.build(type: "transformer", name: "my_model", if_exists: "resume")
model.train(data)   // first run: trains fresh. later reruns: resumes, doesn't retrain from zero
model.weights.get(layer: "layer3", index: [5])   // checking a weight mid-script is just a normal method call
```
Relational/self-organizing weights — an honest, open research direction, not a solved feature. Today's models mostly do NOT cleanly separate concepts into distinct regions on their own — concepts overlap and tangle by default (Section 78's superposition point). Getting weights to naturally organize into clean, mergeable "relationship regions" is a real, active, unsolved research area (disentangled representation learning). Worth attempting, building on Self-Organizing Maps and Graph Neural Networks (Section 32), but must be framed honestly as an attempt at an open problem, not a guarantee:
```
type RelationalWeights extends Technique {
    fn forward(input) { ... }
    fn organize_by_relationship() { /* attempts to cluster related concepts physically closer together */ }
    fn on_new_information(fact) {
        if contradicts_existing(fact) { retrain_region(affected_region, fact) }
        else { merge_into_region(fact, nearest_region) }
    }
}
```
Autonomous research + self-retrain — genuinely buildable from existing pieces, but the highest-risk combination in the system (compounds autonomous data-gathering with autonomous weight change):
```
model.logic {
    input(topic)
    if model.knowledge_map().has_gap(topic) {
        let research = qai.web_search(topic)
        model.online_learn(from: research, budget: "$5", require_review: true)   // mandatory, not optional, for this pattern
    }
    output(model.generate(topic))
}
```
