The single most powerful (and most dangerous) thing logic enables — an AI that designs new AIs when its current architecture isn't enough:
```
model.logic {
    input(persistent_gap)
    if model.knowledge_map().gap_unsolvable_with_current_architecture(persistent_gap) {
        let candidate_types = qai.propose_new_architectures(problem: persistent_gap)
        let results = qai.search_logic_variants(candidates: candidate_types, evaluate_on: relevant_data)
        if results.best().significantly_better_than(model) {
            model.swap_logic(results.best())
            model.log("designed and adopted a new architecture for: " + persistent_gap)
        }
    }
    output(model.generate(persistent_gap))
}
```
This combines gap detection, automated architecture search (Section 68), logic swapping, and safety logging into one loop — genuinely adjacent to automated architecture research. Must always pair with Section 77's safety mechanisms (budget caps, mandatory human review before adopting a new architecture, reward-hacking checks) — the more powerful the capability, the more load-bearing that pairing becomes, not less.
