94. Automated Scientist & Advanced Logic Patterns
An automated research loop — real, testable hypotheses checked against real evidence, not imagination, with mandatory review before internalizing anything:
```
let scientist = qai.build(type: "transformer")
scientist.set_mutability("mutable")

scientist.logic {
    input(research_question)
    let hypothesis = scientist.generate(research_question, mode: "hypothesis")
    let sim = qai.connect_vm(sandboxed: true)   // or a domain-specific simulator/proof-checker
    let test_result = sim.run(hypothesis.to_experiment())

    if test_result.supports(hypothesis) {
        scientist.online_learn(from: { research_question, hypothesis, evidence: test_result }, require_review: true)
    } else {
        let revised = scientist.generate(research_question, mode: "hypothesis", ruled_out: hypothesis)
        // loop, or hand a negative result to a human researcher — a negative result is still real knowledge
    }
    output({ hypothesis, test_result, confidence: scientist.confidence(hypothesis) })
}
```
Directly in line with real, current research on AI-assisted hypothesis generation/testing. The hard, honest part is the "evidence" step — ensuring the system can't fool itself or be fooled by a flawed test — exactly the reward-hacking risk from Section 77, with real-world stakes if conclusions are trusted unchecked.
Fast/slow dual-speed logic — a cheap reactive layer plus a slow deliberate layer engaged only when needed, mirroring real dual-process reasoning as an architectural pattern:
```
model.logic {
    input(x)
    let quick = fast_model.generate(x)
    if fast_model.confidence(quick) > 0.9 { output(quick); return }
    output(slow_model.think(x))
}
```
Further novel patterns:
Logic evolution via genetic combination — breed pieces of two decent logic variants into a new one across generations, rather than only flat side-by-side comparison (Section 69)
A "logic consultant" model — reads someone else's logic block and suggests improvements, applying the framework reflexively to itself
Cross-domain logic transplant — automatically adapt a working pattern from one field (e.g. the movie pipeline's retry-until-consistent loop) to a different domain (e.g. iterative code review) without manual rewriting
Time-travel logic debugging — step backward through a long-running session's actual decision points, not just weight history — "why did it choose this branch three steps ago"
