95. Memory System — Full Consolidated Reference
Memory is scattered across several earlier sections; this is the single, unified map. (Cross-references below point to where each piece was originally designed — nothing duplicated, just indexed together.)
1. Three physical tiers (Section 41/79): `weights` (permanent, changes only via training), `working` (live context window, cleared per session), `retrieval` (external, searchable, fast to update without retraining).
2. Context-window mechanics (Section 53): sliding window, RoPE/position tracking, KV cache, attention distance, decay/fade at the window's edge.
3. Low-memory loading (Section 40): layer streaming, prefetching, disk-as-overflow, partial GPU/CPU split — how the `weights` tier survives constrained hardware.
4. Region-based dynamic loading (Section 76): loading only the relevant weight region per request via a router core.
5. Smart triage (Section 76): relevance scoring, archiving to external files, deletion.
6. Short-term / long-term / discard system (new, sits on top of the above):
```
model.logic {
    input(interaction)
    let relevance = model.memory.relevance(interaction)
    if relevance < 0.2 { model.memory.discard(interaction) }
    else if relevance < 0.6 { model.memory.short_term.add(interaction) }
    else { model.memory.long_term.add(interaction) }
    output(model.generate(interaction, context: model.memory.short_term.recent(3)))
}

model.memory.short_term.on_repeated_reference(fn(item, times_referenced) {
    if times_referenced >= 3 { model.memory.promote(item, to: "long_term") }
})
model.memory.short_term.set_decay(after: "1 hour" | "end_of_session")
model.memory.long_term.set_decay("never")
model.memory.short_term.set_capacity(max_items: 20, on_overflow: "drop_oldest" | "drop_least_relevant")
model.memory.long_term.set_capacity(max_items: 10000, on_overflow: "archive_to_file")
model.memory.long_term.add(interaction, tags: ["preferences","personal"])
model.memory.search(query: "favorite color", tags: ["preferences"])   // searches BOTH tiers, filtered by tag
```
Attaching/detaching multiple memory types on one model — a real, developer-controlled choice, worked example:
```
let model = qai.build(type: "transformer")

model.memory.attach("short_term")                 // enable working-style, session-scoped memory
model.memory.attach("long_term")                    // enable persistent, retrieval-style memory
model.memory.attach("shared_pool", source: qai.connect("shared_memory_db"))   // join a memory pool other models also use

model.logic {
    input(x)
    if x.needs_recent_context { model.memory.detach("long_term") }   // temporarily disable a tier for this exchange
    output(model.generate(x))
    model.memory.attach("long_term")                                    // reattach afterward
}
```
Each tier is genuinely optional and independently toggleable — a model can run with zero memory attached (pure stateless prediction), just `short_term`, just `long_term`, or any combination, decided explicitly by the developer, not assumed.
New ideas (not yet elsewhere in the doc):
Memory "mood" inheritance — emotional/context weight tagged at save-time carries forward on recall
Cross-model shared long-term memory pool (shown above via `shared_pool`)
`model.memory.long_term.export("memories.json")` — portable memory file, separable from weights (e.g. share a companion's personality/weights but let someone start with fresh memory)
`model.memory.usage_report()` — what fraction of long-term memory is ever actually retrieved vs. sitting unused
`model.memory.schedule_review(every: "1 week", fn(item) {...})` — periodic re-evaluation of old memories against current relevance
`model.memory.overview()` — one command surfacing all six pieces' current state at once (window fill, loading strategy, active regions, short/long-term counts, retrieval size)
A Studio panel (Section 93) showing all six pieces as one connected live diagram
