96. Tokenizers & What Actually Comes With a Downloaded Model
Downloading a real model (e.g. from Hugging Face) never gets you just weights — it's a bundle of several distinct files, worth naming explicitly since "download a transformer" hides real complexity:
```
qai.load("huggingface://some-model")
// pulls down, as a bundle:
//   - weights (the actual learned numbers)
//   - tokenizer (rules for splitting text into tokens — often its OWN separate trained artifact)
//   - vocabulary file (the full list of known tokens/subwords)
//   - config (architecture details: layer count, hidden size, etc. — needed to even load the weights correctly)
//   - special tokens (e.g. [START], [END], [PAD], [UNK] — reserved markers the model expects)
//   - model card (a README-style doc: what it's for, training data, known limitations)
```
Tokenizer, explicitly, as its own first-class object — not just a hidden detail:
```
let tokenizer = model.tokenizer()
tokenizer.encode("hello world")     // → token IDs, e.g. [15339, 1917]
tokenizer.decode([15339, 1917])      // → "hello world"
tokenizer.vocab_size()                 // how many distinct tokens it knows
tokenizer.special_tokens()              // → { start: "<s>", end: "</s>", pad: "<pad>", unknown: "<unk>" }
```
Why this matters practically: a tokenizer is trained specifically alongside its model — using a mismatched tokenizer with a model's weights silently produces garbage, since token ID 15339 means something completely different across two different tokenizers. Worth an explicit compatibility check:
```
qai.check_tokenizer_match(model, tokenizer)
// → "Match confirmed" or → "Warning: this tokenizer was not trained with this model — results will be unreliable"
```
