99. Numerical Correctness, Evaluation Integrity & Alignment Techniques
Automatic differentiation as the real fix for hand-written gradient bugs — the compiler derives `update()` from `forward()`/`objective()` directly, rather than requiring anyone to write backprop math by hand:
```
type MyTechnique {
    fn forward(x) { return dot(w, x) + b }
    fn objective(output, target) { return (output - target)^2 }
    #[autodiff]   // update() auto-derived — an entire bug class (wrong chain-rule application) becomes structurally impossible
}
qai.numerics.check_gradient(MyTechnique, test_case)     // finite-difference verification, feeds into validate{} (Section 56)
model.train(data, gradient_clipping: "auto", stability_check: true)
qai.numerics.detect_instability(training_run)              // live exploding/vanishing gradient detection
```
Numerics operate on the underlying tensor math in `forward()`, independent of schema field names — a custom `input_schema`/`output_schema` composes correctly with autodiff automatically, since the schema only defines shape, not the math itself.
Data leakage prevented structurally, not just by convention — `TestSet` is its own restricted type:
```
let (train_set, test_set) = qai.dataset.split(data, train: 0.8, test: 0.2)
test_set.evaluate(model)      // ✅ allowed
test_set.train(model)          // ❌ compile-time error — TestSet has no train() method at all
qai.detect_leakage(train_set, test_set)   // scans for overlapping/near-duplicate examples
```
Alignment as a first-class post-training method, called after `build()`/`train()`, alongside `finetune()` — not a build-time argument:
```
let model = qai.build(type: "transformer", learning_technique: "supervised")
model.train(data)
model.align(method: "rlhf", preference_data: pairs)
model.align(method: "dpo", preference_data: pairs)
model.align(method: "constitutional", principles: [...])
model.align(method: "adversarial_training", attack_data: adversarial_examples)
model.align(method: "debiasing", protected_attributes: [...])
model.align(method: "refusal_tuning", refuse_on: [...])
```
Connects directly to existing measurement tools (bias scanner Section 26, adversarial stress-testing Section 26, guardrails Section 17) — `align()` is where a model is actually trained to internalize what those tools measure.
Standing documentation rule (binding from here forward): every new method introduced must carry a real use case, following the `qai.man()` four-part shape (meaning, impact, example, why it exists) — no method added just because it sounds plausible. A full retroactive audit of all earlier sections against this rule is real, valuable, and not yet done — treated as a distinct future cleanup pass, same category as Section 83's consistency audit.
Runtime portability, confirmed: the embedded runtime bundled in a `.qmodel` export is built from the same Rust→C toolchain as the language itself — small and self-contained by construction, so "any environment with enough RAM can run it" is an honest claim, not aspirational.
