105. VM Realism, Project Integration & Developer Notebook
Corrected self-play syntax (fixes a drift from Section 14's established design — a self-play system uses roles/connections, not a bare `train(evaluator: fn)` call):
```
let model = qai.build("combined")
model.add_role("coder", type: "transformer")
model.add_role("reviewer", type: "classifier")
model.connect("coder" -> "reviewer", channel: "code")
model.set_evaluator(fn(code) { return vm.run(code).tests_passed && reviewer.check(code) })
model.train(mode: "self_play", rounds: 1000)
```
Realistic VM mechanics — bootstrap, access-vs-competence, lazy install, persistence:
```
let vm = qai.connect_vm(type: "full_container", os: "ubuntu")
// first ever call: no container backend present → downloads/installs one (e.g. Firecracker) automatically, caches the base image
// every future call: instant, already present
```
Access ≠ competence — enabling VM capabilities gives a model access; using them well is a separate, trainable skill:
```
let vm_skill = qai.skill.train(base: model, data: qai.dataset.stream("huggingface://shell_command_examples"), name: "vm_navigation")
model.attach_skill(vm_skill)
```
Lazy, on-demand package installation — never eager, only installs what running code actually requires:
```
vm.run(code)   // installs numpy automatically ONLY if the code imports it — no upfront install list needed
```
Persistent VM state across sessions:
```
vm.save_state()   // installed packages, cached image, created files preserved
// next session picks up exactly where it left off
```
Backend choice, since different container backends have real tradeoffs:
```
qai.connect_vm(type: "full_container", backend: "firecracker" | "docker" | "gvisor")
```
Project-folder integration — VM state lives inside the same project structure as everything else (Section 42):
```
project/
├── models/   ├── logic/   ├── data/
├── vm/                      // persistent VM state: packages, cached image, created files
└── notebook/                 // developer's own notes (below)

qai.package_project("my_project")   // bundles EVERYTHING, including VM state, as one portable unit
```
Developer notebook — distinct from automated system logs; captures what the developer noticed/thought/decided, not what the system did:
```
project.notebook.add_note("Found the model ignores negation words — investigating layer 4")
project.notebook.log_error(error, context: "during finetune on 2026-08-30")
project.notebook.log_security_finding("VM allowed an unexpected network call — tightening allowlist")
project.notebook.history()
project.notebook.search("negation")
```
