106. Experiment Tracking Dashboard & Paged KV Cache Serving
Experiment tracking — confirmed via real 2026 industry research as the single most consistently prioritized MLOps capability ("the glue" of the ML lifecycle: a trustworthy record of what was trained, with which data and code, and what came out). The top real gap in the whole design, now fixed:
```
qai.experiments.track(model, run_name: "regression_v3_lr0.01",
    tags: ["baseline","learning_rate_sweep"],
    log: { hyperparameters: model.hyperparameters(), data_version: dataset.version_hash(), code_version: qai.git_hash() }
)

qai.experiments.dashboard()
// → sortable, filterable table of every run: name, tags, hyperparameters, accuracy, loss, timestamp, linked dataset/code version

qai.experiments.compare(["regression_v3_lr0.01", "regression_v3_lr0.001"])   // side-by-side, any two or more runs
qai.experiments.best(metric: "accuracy", filter: { tags: "learning_rate_sweep" })
```
This ties together and gives a real home to results from `.train()`, `qai.compare_significance()` (Section 55), and `model.compare_logic()` (Section 69) — the dedicated surface for "what have I actually tried, and what worked," which nothing before this centralized.
Paged KV cache management for serving many simultaneous requests — distinct from Section 53's per-conversation KV cache handling, addressing real concurrent-load efficiency:
```
qai.serve(model, kv_cache_strategy: "paged")
// manages KV cache memory in small, reusable blocks shared across ALL simultaneous requests,
// instead of each conversation reserving its own fixed-size chunk — a real, named serving technique (PagedAttention-style),
// not a per-conversation optimization
```
