110. Checkpoint Management, Training Mechanics & Data Handling
```
model.train(data, save_total_limit: 3, load_best_model_at_end: true, metric_for_best_model: "eval_accuracy")

model.train(data, weight_decay: 0.01, weight_decay_exclude: ["bias","layer_norm"])   // applying decay to bias/norm params often hurts training

tokenizer.set_pad_token_if_missing("<pad>")   // common gotcha — some tokenizers have no default pad token
tokenizer.pad(dynamic: true)                    // pad each batch to its own longest example, not a fixed length

qai.dataset.stream(source, shuffle_buffer_size: 10000)   // rolling-buffer shuffle approximation for infinite streams

model.train(data, on_nan_loss: "skip_batch" | "stop_training")

model.train(data, batch_size: 8, gradient_accumulation_steps: 4, num_devices: 2)
model.effective_batch_size()   // → 64, shown explicitly since it's easy to miscalculate

qai.load("huggingface://some-model", attention_implementation: "flash_attention_2"|"eager", torch_dtype: "bf16")
qai.load(model_path, ignore_mismatched_sizes: true)   // for loading a pretrained model with a changed output head
```
Notably important: weight-decay exclusion and the missing-pad-token issue are common, real causes of subtly-worse training that people often only learn about the hard way — worth flagging in docs as common-mistake warnings, not just optional flags.
