112. Class Balance, Evaluation Metrics & Learning-Rate Tuning
```
qai.dataset.check_class_balance(data)   // → "Class 'fraud': 0.3% of data — severely imbalanced"
data.balance(method: "oversample"|"undersample"|"smote")

model.confusion_matrix(normalize: "true"|"pred"|"all"|"none")
model.accuracy(top_k: 5)   // "was the right answer in the top 5 guesses"

qai.find_learning_rate(model, data)   // trains briefly across a rate range, plots loss vs. rate, suggests the steepest-descent point

model.train(data, gradient_accumulation_steps: 4, scale_loss_by_accumulation: true)   // prevents an accidentally too-aggressive effective batch

model.export(..., pin_environment: true)   // records exact qai/runtime version, catches "works on my machine" failures later

model.generate(prompt, stop_sequences: ["\n\n","END","###"])

model.predict_batch(inputs, batch_size: "auto")   // probes memory, picks the largest safe batch size itself
```
Notably important: the learning-rate finder — guessing a learning rate is one of the most common early-training mistakes, and this removes a frequent source of wasted runs.
