113. Loss Functions, Per-Layer Rates & Inference Optimization
```
model.train(data, loss_fn: "cross_entropy", label_smoothing: 0.1)
model.train(data, loss_fn: "focal_loss", focal_gamma: 2.0)   // for severe class imbalance, focuses on hard examples

model.train(data, layer_lr: { "embedding": 0.0001, "classifier_head": 0.001 })   // different parts learn at different speeds

model.train(data, metric_smoothing: "ema", ema_decay: 0.9)   // smooths noisy epoch-to-epoch validation fluctuation

qai.numerics.set_cuda_deterministic(true)   // slower, but bit-for-bit reproducible on the same hardware (ties to Section 103)

model.tokenizer.trim_vocab(keep_tokens_used_in: my_dataset)   // shrink vocabulary to only what's actually needed

model.predict_batch(inputs, preserve_order: true)   // guarantees output[i] matches input[i] even if processed out of order internally

qai.inference_mode {
    let result = model.predict(x)   // skips gradient tracking entirely — faster, less memory, pure-inference code
}
```
Notably important: `inference_mode` — running predictions without gradient tracking is one of the most common real production performance wins, and its absence would be a genuine practical gap.
