114. Hardware-Burned Export: Chip Detection, Types & Reversibility
Most AI models never change weights after training — that's the normal, universal default everywhere, including most Quantum models. Mutable weights via `logic` (Section 75) are a deliberate, opt-in exception, not the norm, and this section's chip-export options sit at the far end of "permanent" on that same spectrum.
Chip detection and honest capability reporting:
```
qai.detect_chip()
// → { type: "flash", model: "ESP32-S3", storage_available: "4MB", supports_erase: true }
```
Export targets, by real reversibility:
```
model.export(target: "chip", chip_type: "flash")     // erasable/rewritable (like an ESP32, Coral Edge TPU, Pi Pico) — same technology as a USB drive or SD card, just built into the device
model.export(target: "chip", chip_type: "fpga")        // reconfigurable at the circuit-logic level, not just weights — most flexible, more costly/power-hungry
model.export(target: "chip", chip_type: "asic")          // fabricated permanently into silicon — genuinely irreversible, no erase option ever offered for this type
```
`qai.chip.erase()` / `qai.chip.reflash(new_model)` are only ever offered when `supports_erase: true` — the interface itself reflects hardware truth rather than exposing an option that would silently fail on permanent silicon.
Hybrid path — iterate on flash, only "graduate" to permanent ASIC once genuinely stable:
```
model.chip_readiness_check()
// → "This model has changed 12 times in the last 30 days — NOT recommended for permanent ASIC burning yet.
//    Recommend flash deployment until stability improves."
```
ASIC/fixed-function burning is a real, valuable, narrow option specifically for a model that is final and never needs to change — a fixed edge-device task (a doorbell's person-detector) — and is fundamentally incompatible with anything using `logic`, mutable weights, skills, or online learning.
