115. Strategic Critique — Standing Notes
Captured here as durable project notes, not features to build, since these are risks about the process of building this, not gaps in the design itself:
API surface size — 113+ sections is more than any one person can hold in their head; discoverability at this scale needs either a much smaller, aggressively-surfaced "80% of real use" core, or genuinely strong search — neither is stress-tested yet.
Unverified core claims — "faster than Python," "compiles to C," "5 lines instead of 100" are the actual value proposition and have never been benchmarked against anything real.
Untested feature interactions — mutable weights + distributed training + self-modifying logic + dynamic region loading were each designed individually; how they behave together, under real concurrent load, has never been examined.
No defined MVP — the single most important missing artifact is not a feature, it's a written answer to "what ships first, by when."
No named primary audience — total beginners and self-modifying-architecture researchers have contradictory needs; v1 needs to pick one.
No adoption plan for the first real users, independent of the marketplace/registry tooling that assumes a community already exists.
Novel vs. valuable, conflated — a meaningful share of the design is a clean recombination of existing ideas (fine — most good engineering is), but the genuinely differentiated core (open `Technique`/`Type` interface discipline, logic as a real language construct, mutable weights via logic) deserves to stay visible as distinct from the rest.
No continuity plan — what state would the project be in if development paused for six months; is there enough decision-level documentation (not just feature documentation) for someone else to pick it up.
